\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Peru Sol Has Room to Gain Within Tight Range -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,538 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2013\par 19:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 13:36 EDT - USD/PEN has a natural ceiling of around PEN2.80 to PEN2.83 per US dollar, and if that is tested, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  says the \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in sol weakness. Firm sees the sol strengthening to PEN2.75 a dollar, from its current 2.77 level. Upbeat data out of China and new investment projects in Peru should support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it adds, though too much strength could also stir up \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely manage the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a relatively narrow range, preventing too much PEN strength to maintain export competitiveness, and restrict excessive PEN weakness to protect businesses with (unhedged) USD-denominated liabilities," \uc2 HSBC\uc2  says. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:22 EDT - Of the crazy mortgages issued during the housing bubble, possibly the most egregious was pick-a-pay. Such loans allowed borrowers to decide how much to pay monthly (of course not paying the full minimum increased the loan balance). That California-based favorite helped push \uc2 Wachovia\uc2 --which inherited such loans through a top-of-the-market aquisition--into \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 's (WFC) arms. The bank breaks out some numbers for such mortgages in its quarterly results, and the picture continues to brighten. WFC's pick-a-pay balances were $29.4B as of Sept. 30, down from $32B to start 2013; the total topped $120B under \uc2 Wachovia\uc2 . And loan-to-value levels keep falling, with such California borrowers on average having 2% equity in their home versus being 13% underwater at year's end. Thanks, rebounding house prices. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:22 EDT - US stocks once again chip away at early weakness, major indexes all plow back into positive territory. Investors exhibit an almost zealous refusal to entertain the strengthening possibility of a failure to lift the debt limit, and continue to wager that a deal will appear before the deadline. Tech and energy sector lead the advance, while utilities, telecom and health-care are still down. \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , 3M, \uc2 Chevron\uc2  and Visa help the Dow Industrials erase an earlier 100-point drop. DJIA up 20; Nasdaq rises 11; S&P 500 up 2. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:05 EDT - Signs of longer-term technical deterioration are starting to worry some bullish chart watchers. \uc2 Auerbach Grayson\uc2  technician Richard Ross said the market appears headed higher short-term as investors believe a deal to extend the debt ceiling will eventually be reached. But he's becoming "deeply concerned" that while the S&P 500 made two higher record highs since May, a closely-watched momentum indicator -- the Relative Strength Index -- made two lower highs on the weekly charts over the same time, and turned lower again this week. He said there's an "ominous technical symmetry" between the current bearish RSI divergence and the one in 2011 that preceded a 19% drop in the S&P 500. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:59 EDT - Will free appliances, splitting closing costs, or maybe just a new garage-door opener be enough to lure buyers into the housing market? These are some of the financial carrots The WSJ reports homebuilders are offering as a recent industry downturn continues, no doubt aided by the government shutdown. S&P Homebuilders Select Industry Index is down 4.4% in the last 3 months, against a 1.6% fall on the DJIA. KBW notes that October is typically a tough month for homebuilding stocks, but expects the usual outperformance after that ahead of Spring selling season. (joe.melvin@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:32 EDT - Whirlpool (WHR) shares slide as customers step back from big ticket purchases amid uncertainty surrounding the government shutdown and debt ceiling debate in Washington. Channel checks by \uc2 Longbow Research\uc2  show customers delaying purchases in the last week of September and into October as the debt ceiling deadline approaches. A pullback in WHR is not a big surprise as shares have gained 58% over the last year as appliance purchases returned from the recession. Longbow does expect purchases to rebound once the landscape clears in Washington, though some deals may just be lost as consumers decided to repair rather than replace appliances. WHR down 5.7% at $132.40. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:26 EDT - Just like last week, anything that smells like compromise in Washington pushes stocks higher. The Dow industrials is down only 30 points after falling as many as 101 earlier while cyclical S&P 500 sectors are nearly flat and the Nasdaq Composite has edged into the green after falling as many as 25. The move comes as Obama is set to meet with House and Senate leaders at 3pm, and Biden will also take part. Traders are also passing around a Politico story that Reid has made an offer to McConnell which would provide short-term measures to both raise the debt limit and reopen the government. (christopher.dieterich@wsj.com; @chrisdieterich)\par \par \uc2 11:55 EDT - Penn National's (PENN) parts could be worth considerably more than the $56 stock price, with Susquehanna suggesting investors may not have fully factored in the potential accretion of M&A activity post-spin. Firm sees "less controversy" surrounding the operating company, thinks it could pursue acquisitions of smaller casino operators and gaming management contracts; raises to positive from neutral. The casino operator is spinning off its real estate assets in 4Q, with each PENN holder receiving cash dividend, NewCo shares. NewCo, Gaming & Leisure Properties, may initially struggle due to weak regional gaming fundamentals, but Susquehanna sees bounce once investors become more comfortable with reliability of gaming cash flows, operating strategy. Shares up 1.2% at $56.16. (joe.melvin@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:28 EDT - We're going on two weeks without CFTC flow data because of the government shutdown, but if \uc2 UBS\uc2  client data is any indication, there may not be much position shifting in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to report anyway. The investment bank reports \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows stalling last week, with dollar volumes "collapsing" to 77% of its average and euro and sterling activity even weaker. "Not putting trades on appeared to be the preferred expression of risk aversion," \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. Those who were around were mainly real-money asset managers, mostly selling the dollar against euro and yen. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 11:15 EDT - The closer a company's quarterly report gets, the less optimistic analysts generally become about that period's earnings. Take 3Q, for which S&P 500 firms' profits at the start of the year were seen rising 11%, according to Thomson Reuters. That fell to 8.5% at mid-year and 4.6% on Oct. 1 amid 5 times as many big caps giving downbeat bottom-line guidance for 3Q than upbeat views. While realizing just a smattering of S&P 500 firms have reported thus far (29 versus 69 alone slated for this week and another 146 next week), Thomson Reuters is wondering whether despite the weak sentiment coming into earnings season, this will be a rare case of Wall Street not cutting numbers enough as the miss rate is running higher than normal. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:05 EDT - Touch PCs aren't a big hit so far, leading Pacific Crest to cut Atmel (ATML) -- maker of chips for touch screens -- to sector perform from outperform. "On our recent trip to Asia, we encountered widespread skepticism from the PC supply chain regarding the current outlook for the ramp of touch PCs, despite a reset in expectations earlier this year," firm says. "While our initial concerns centered around supply issues, supply-chain partners are indicating that sell-through of touch PCs has been disappointing." Pacific Crest looks for flat 4Q13 revenue growth at ATML "to reflect higher levels of touch PC inventories and the suspension of various touch PC launches by OEMs." Shares off 0.7% at $7.34. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:47 EDT - Inflation in India is once again mounting, with consumer and wholesale price indexes rising above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's comfort level. Reduction in fuel subsidies and higher food prices contributed to the spike. Now, India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  faces a tough balancing act as it tries to stimulate falling growth while keeping prices in check. The rupee's stability in recent weeks gives the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  room to unwind some of the tightening measures it introduced this summer to curb the rupee's depreciation. But the country's trade imbalances and weak economic outlook will threaten to weaken the rupee in the coming months, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  analysts say. The dollar up 0.7% to INR61.45. (prabha.natarajan@wsj.com; @prabhanatarajan) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 14, 2013 13:36 ET (17:36 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131014e9ae00382\par 