\par\par \b \uc2 Peru Sol to Find Stability from  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  - Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,373 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2013\par 18:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:41 EDT - \uc2 BBVA\uc2  Banco Continental sees Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ending this year at around PEN2.76 per US dollar, a slight appreciation from its current level at PEN2.78. Like other emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sol has depreciated sharply this year due to expectations that the US Federal Reserve will start pulling back on its bond-buying program. BBVA chief economist Hugo Perea said he expects \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market to prevent volatility. "If it sees very intense volatility, it will likely continue to sell dollars and it has a lot of liquidity to do so," he said. (ryan.dube@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:23 EDT - \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  admits this summer's sharp rise in yields is overdone in the short-term, but that doesn't mean the firm has shifted its bearish longer-term stance. Goldman increased forecasts on the 10-year bund and gilt yields to reflect the recent string of positive data out of Europe. The adjustments suggest yields will end the year around current levels, but trend higher in 2014. Goldman now sees the 10-year bund yielding 2% at the end of 2013 and 2.5% at the end of 2014, from previous predictions of 1.8% and 2%, respectively. The 10-year gilt yield is expected to end at 2.75% this year and 3% next, from 2.3% and 2.6%, respectively. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 12:05 EDT - BofA/Merrill sees the euro falling to $1.25 at the end of the year. The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fetches $1.33 now, which the dealer says isn't justified by its region's economic conditions. Firm points to high unemployment and low inflation in the euro zone relative to the US, which should be weighing on the euro. Meanwhile, the US dollar's short- and long-term future looks bright with the Fed about to pull the tapering trigger. And because many euro bears have given in ever since the ECB last year said it's determined to keep the euro intact, BofA says there's plenty of room for those shorts to rebuild. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 11:54 EDT - Mortgage applications are continuing to slide--with ones for refinancings hitting a 4-year low last week, the Mortgage Bankers Association said this morning. A reflection of higher interest rates, Steve Blitz of ITG Investment Research says Fed officials should take note. If the data are "any indication of what's going on in housing at the moment--and it usually is--the bulls on the FOMC can't be feeling quite so confident in their acceleration assessment of the US economy." (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:48 EDT - Verizon's record $49B debt offering, expected to price around noon, is complicating dealers' routine effort to bring down Treasury prices before a \uc2 US government\uc2  note sale. The US sells $21B in 10-year notes at 1pm EDT. Rick Klingman, senior trader at primary dealer \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 , says he will wait until Verizon's bonds are priced before setting up for the 10-year sale. Usually dealers sell Treasurys in the morning on auction days to prepare for the supply. But today, Klingman says he is concerned that setting up too early could mean missing out on a potential rally if hedges linked to the Verizon offering unwinds. The 10-year notes up 2/32 to yield 2.959%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 11:29 EDT - July's industrial output falls 0.5% on year, which is less than expected and comes on top of weak retail data. More discouraging economic signals like this could provide an argument for another interest-rate cut, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . "Given the benign inflation outlook...and increasing slack in the economy, we are of the view that if data continues to print on the weak side, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may cut again at the October 25 meeting," the firm says. The \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  cut its policy rate by 25bps last week to 3.75%. (laurence.iliff@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1519 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Erste Bank expects volatility on the Hungarian market to be elevated in the coming weeks and months after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Gyorgy Matolcsy said there is further room to cut the 3.8% policy rate. He said it was underpinned by the "clear improvement" in the macroeconomic situation, and his expectation that loose monetary conditions in the world can prevail over the medium term. Erste economist Zoltan Arokszallasi maintains his EUR/HUF forecast of 302.5 at the end of 2013 versus the current level of 299.12. He expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut the base rate to 3.5% by the year-end, and likely by around 10 bps at the upcoming September meeting. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1457 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Hungarian bond yields drop slightly as comments by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Gyorgy Matolcsy on continuing rate cuts can't fully offset the impact of surprisingly successful switch auction this morning. The curve flattens further as yields drop by more in longer maturities. The 3-year benchmark bond yield is 5.12%-5.02%, the 5-year is 5.73%-5.63% and the 10-year is 6.40%-6.30%. Markets' reception of Mr. Matolcsy's announcement is mixed as the easing cycle puts the forint under pressure. (veronika.gulyas@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1440 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The 'risk premium' attached to the new Verizon bonds has already been wiped out, says Marc Ostwald at Monument Securities, if there was any to start with. Verizon new bonds are trading in the grey market way tighter than their launch. It looks like there are many more people worried about getting a chunk of these bonds at almost any cost, since it will weigh massively on their indexes, rather than worrying about a 'risk premium', he says. Every tranche is 20-30 basis points tighter in the grey market, with the new 30-year trading 40 bps tighter at 225 bps vs 265 bps over Treasurys at launch. (serena.ruffoni@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:24 EDT - Nomura says that Verizon's (VZ) massive bond and debt issue is coming at a better interest rate than expected adding to possible earnings accretion in 2014 and supporting its positive outlook on the shares. From press reports, Nomura sees a weighted-average interest rate of 4.4%, compared to early expectations for 4.9%. Nomura says concerns over the amount of equity financing being sold into the market are valid, but it expects them to be only temporary. VZ little changed at $46.46 (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:23 EDT - Bond yields shot up Tuesday on hedges linked to Verizon's (VZ) blockbuster debt offering, and with that record deal expected to be priced at noon, unwinding those hedges could send Treasury yields lower, some traders say. But the VZ pricing comes an hour ahead of Treasury's $21B 10-year sale, so that makes it tricky for dealers to set up for the government auction. There's "a huge unknown that could be priced before the auction or could be priced after the auction," says Thomas Roth at Mitsubishi UFJ. Nonetheless, Nomura expects a "solid" 10-year auction as it will "attract value investors." (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 10:18 EDT - The dollar just took a dip against the euro and yen, driven by a set of options positions that expired at 10am EDT, according to a New York-based \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a major investment bank. Investors had been slightly long the dollar in recent weeks, and continue to pare those positions ahead of next week's Fed meeting, the trader said. Euro jumped to as high as $1.3302 just before the 10am expiry, and traded recently at $1.3291, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar slipped as low as Y99.83m recently at Y100.01. (matthew.walter@wsj.com; @mbwalter4) [ 11-09-13 1641GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130911e99b000k8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
