\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: Treasurys Not the Haven It Used to Be\b0\par\par\uc2 1,322 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2013\par 21:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Treasurys are losing a bit of their safe-haven appeal. While we haven't seen the likes of Greece or Cyprus recently that could still spark a flight into Treasurys, Columbia Asset's \uc2 Brian Weisman\uc2  says the safety of this classic haven market is being defined differently now. He says investors are conscious of the damaging effects of rising rates, and that it's been a while since a client has asked to be invested in "pure safety" and with little regard for yield. In this environment, Weisman says the 10-year yield will have to get closer to 4% before "it would start to register on my radar" as an investment consideration for his clients. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 A confluence of forces are working against US Treasurys. Fed tapering fears have been the main driver, but traders say the technical break of Thursday's 2.823% peak triggered even more selling. Higher yields also spur more hedging from mortgage bond holders, traders say, which puts added pressure on Treasurys. This is all happening in thin August liquidity, which magnifies swings. "Clearly the momentum trade is in control," says \uc2 Kevin Giddis\uc2 , head of fixed income at Raymond James. And while rate strategists at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  say the market looks oversold with the 10-year yield reaching its 2.84%-2.915% target zone, the march higher in rates still looks intact. Ten-year notes down 25/32, yielding 2.858%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 When Treasury yields rise, riskier assets like stocks and emerging-market assets tend to shine as well. But not this time. As it is the prospect of Fed tapering that sparked a selloff in Treasury bonds, the higher yields have driven investors to sell US stocks as they prepare for a less generous Fed. No wonder the bond king has the blues. In a message Friday on Pimco's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account, \uc2 Bill Gross\uc2  says that all asset markets are peaking and that without \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  check writing, or pumping money from QEs, "we only have ourselves [to] sell [to]." (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 In a survey of its members, one of Venezuela's largest private-sector groups, Conindustria, estimates that the countries' industries are working at little more than half capacity. Among the main reasons given for the stunted output were political uncertainty, the difficulty in obtaining foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and raw materials, and power outages. President Nicolas Maduro, barely out of his first 100 days in office, began his term by reaching out to the private sector and naming cabinet members that promised more pragmatic policy. But political tension remains high and dollars are still scarce in the stalled economy. (ezequiel.minaya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Technical factors are coming into play on a thinned out Friday session, with few buyers around to stem the bleeding in US Treasurys. Benchmark 10-year yields just rose another 3bps, now at 2.851%, setting yet another 2-year high. Technical strategists have warned that breaching 2.75% would pave the way to faster selling. The next key support seen luring in buyers is just above 3%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Underscoring how sentiment toward bonds remains negative, Treasurys are getting hit with another round of selling after stabilizing this morning. The 10-year yield climbs as high as 2.829%, topping the 2-year high of 2.823% set Thursday. "The long-term trend is higher in Treasury yields," says \uc2 Sean Simko\uc2 , fixed-income manager at \uc2 SEI Investments\uc2 . "Anytime there is a strong move higher in Treasury yields, there will be certain investors that will reallocate assets out of bond funds." Simko says bond prices are looking more attractive in the short-term after this recent selloff. "If there is any inclination that the Fed may not taper in September, Treasury bonds could catch a strong bid," he adds. Ten-year notes down 16/32, yielding 2.825%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 Pimco senior rate strategist Tony Crescenzi writes in his August missive three policy takeaways that will influence the bond market in the years to come. 1) It takes cumulative data for the Fed to act. 2) Tapering is not tightening. In fact, Crescenzi notes the expected $10B reduction in monthly MBS purchases will actually be less than MBS originations because of the recent plunge in mortgage refinancing activity. That means at $30B a month, the Fed would actually be buying a bigger proportion of the market. 3) QE and the policy rate are two separate tools. As investors grow to understand this, Crescenzi says rates will be tied more to the latter. Pimco see the Fed funds rate unchanged until early 2016. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to ease the depreciation on the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the US dollar, Banco de Credito said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in recent sessions, selling dollars on the spot market. So far this week, it has sold $620M to prevent the sol from weakening further. The sol has depreciated 9.6% against the greenback so far this year, with the dollar recently trading at PEN2.797. (ryan.dube@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 For the sixth-straight week, the amount of US Treasurys owned by foreign accounts stashed at the NY Fed fell. The $5.2B drop brings the total decline since early June to $58B. But RBS assures the streak shouldn't be interpreted as foreign investors losing confidence in \uc2 US government\uc2  bonds. Instead, the firm points out that these so-called custody holdings tend to track how Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  perform. When these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened amid growing Fed stimulus, dollars were bought to cap that strength, then often used to invest in US Treasurys. Now that EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are weakening, the process is reversing. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Peru's economic conditions are putting its policymakers between a rock and a hard place. The \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of Peru\uc2  faces decelerating economic growth, which points to a need for cut rates, while inflation and exchange-rate pressures may require a rate hike instead. "Facing these two opposite forces, we believe the most likely scenario is that the bank will remain on hold," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said. "We think an initial stimulus to contain the slowdown could be provided through additional reductions in reserve requirements." Last week, Peru left its policy rate at 4.25%, where it has stood since May 2011. (ryan.dube@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Disappointing July housing starts earlier this morning and now a consumer sentiment report coming in below expectations aren't usually the makings of a dollar rally. Yet, the greenback is holding up against the yen, which shows how fervently markets believe an autumn tapering is coming. At this point, many market participants believe only a dismal August nonfarm payrolls number can derail a stimulus reduction. Dollar up 0.1% at Y97.48. Euro up 0.1% to $1.3355, but down from $1.3374 just before the sentiment report. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Bond prices hold losses, brushing aside a report that showed consumers feeling less confident this month. But the data doesn't change the prospect of less Fed stimulus, so anxiety over the Fed tapering next month continues to weigh on bonds. A key question now is if the Fed does taper in September, how much will it reduce monthly purchases by? Fed's Bullard floated the idea of a mini-tapering Thursday. If that turns out to be the case, bond bears might be forced to pull back negative bets on bonds, which would help Treasurys regain footing. Ten-year notes down 7/32, yielding 2.792%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj) [ 16-08-13 1907GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130816e98g000ke\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
