\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: \uc2 Peru  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Likely to Keep Supporting Sol\b0\par\par\uc2 1,233 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2013\par 16:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to ease the depreciation on the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the US dollar, Banco de Credito said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in recent sessions, selling dollars on the spot market. So far this week, it has sold $620M to prevent the sol from weakening further. The sol has depreciated 9.6% against the greenback so far this year, with the dollar recently trading at PEN2.797. (ryan.dube@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 For the sixth-straight week, the amount of US Treasurys owned by foreign accounts stashed at the NY Fed fell. The $5.2B drop brings the total decline since early June to $58B. But RBS assures the streak shouldn't be interpreted as foreign investors losing confidence in \uc2 US government\uc2  bonds. Instead, the firm points out that these so-called custody holdings tend to track how Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  perform. When these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened amid growing Fed stimulus, dollars were bought to cap that strength, then often used to invest in US Treasurys. Now that EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are weakening, the process is reversing. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Peru's economic conditions are putting its policymakers between a rock and a hard place. The \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of Peru\uc2  faces decelerating economic growth, which points to a need for cut rates, while inflation and exchange-rate pressures may require a rate hike instead. "Facing these two opposite forces, we believe the most likely scenario is that the bank will remain on hold," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said. "We think an initial stimulus to contain the slowdown could be provided through additional reductions in reserve requirements." Last week, Peru left its policy rate at 4.25%, where it has stood since May 2011. (ryan.dube@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Disappointing July housing starts earlier this morning and now a consumer sentiment report coming in below expectations aren't usually the makings of a dollar rally. Yet, the greenback is holding up against the yen, which shows how fervently markets believe an autumn tapering is coming. At this point, many market participants believe only a dismal August nonfarm payrolls number can derail a stimulus reduction. Dollar up 0.1% at Y97.48. Euro up 0.1% to $1.3355, but down from $1.3374 just before the sentiment report. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Bond prices hold losses, brushing aside a report that showed consumers feeling less confident this month. But the data doesn't change the prospect of less Fed stimulus, so anxiety over the Fed tapering next month continues to weigh on bonds. A key question now is if the Fed does taper in September, how much will it reduce monthly purchases by? Fed's Bullard floated the idea of a mini-tapering Thursday. If that turns out to be the case, bond bears might be forced to pull back negative bets on bonds, which would help Treasurys regain footing. Ten-year notes down 7/32, yielding 2.792%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real, which has been losing ground to the dollar since March, has more room to fall in the coming year, say \uc2 Barclays\uc2  strategists. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness arises from global and local factors. At home, Brazil's government remains reluctant to loosen up fiscal policy, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks as if it's no longer defending a specific exchange rate to help hold up the real, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  notes. All the while, if the Fed does reduce stimulus, less global money will be headed Brazil's way. This should put further pressure on BRL, which is currently trading at a four-year low to the dollar at BRL2.3535. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  sees the dollar rising to BRL2.45 within the next 12 months. (prabha.natarajan@wsj.com; @prabhanatarajan)\par \par \uc2 Foreigners dumped a lot of Canadian bonds--C$18.6B--in June. It's an unexpected slap in the face for a country that's gotten used to having its bond sought after by investors around the world because of Canada's Triple A credit rating and healthy fiscal outlook. But it's instructive to note the selling came in June, when foreign investors were also scrambling to get out of \uc2 US government\uc2  bonds. That suggests the expectation the Fed will "taper" its stimulative bond-buying programs and the concomitant selling of US bonds spilled over the border into Canada, whose economy and markets are inextricably intertwined with those of the US. (don.curren@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:00 EDT - Jefferies is waiting for Venezuela's hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  auctions, launched in March, to offer bonds as promised by authorities and believes that might happen soon. Nicolas Maduro's presidency has not had much of a honeymoon period and recent initiative targeting government corruption has backfired. "This low political capital suggests that the Maduro Administration will have to grow imports to fund domestic consumption and production, financed near-term either through bond issuance or drawing down the stock of non-reserve assets. We view bond issuance as" the best alternative," Jefferies says. (ezequiel.minaya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 CIBC World Markets says flooding in Alberta was not the driver for the 0.5% decline in Canadian manufacturing shipments in June, as that province recorded a 0.1% gain. Instead, weakness originated in Ontario and British Columbia, with sales of fabricated metals and "miscellaneous products" declining. In real terms, sales were down a sharp 1.3%, suggesting a more acute hit to June GDP than was foreshadowed in the earlier merchandise trade report, CIBC says. (don.curren@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The dollar falls to Y97.28 after July housing starts disappoint, from Y97.52 just before the data. But the move isn't a dramatic one--the market believes September tapering is still on the table because traders' faith won't be shaken by a miss in one piece of second-tier data. The miss, however, does contribute to the stagnation the dollar have been facing for the last few weeks: while the market is convinced of a Fed stimulus rollback in September, US data have been spotty and uneven, leaving the dollar and most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  adrift for the time being. Euro up 0.1% to $1.3365.(ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 CAD was bumped a little bit lower after two second-tier data series came in much weaker than expected, with manufacturing shipments in June dropping by 0.05% vs the expected 0.3% increase and foreigners dumping C$15.4B in Canadian securities vs the forecast investment of C$7.35B. USD/CAD hit 1.0331 after the release from 1.0304 late Thursday. USD/CAD is slightly below the midpoint of its 1.0250 to 1.0450 trading range in recent weeks, and is highly unlikely to escape from it in either direction today. (don.curren@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Downward revisions to 1Q and weak 2Q numbers mean productivity is hardly moving so far this year. New data (linked to GDP revisions) show 1Q output per hour in nonfarm business fell 1.7%, instead of a 0.5% gain as first reported, and 2Q productivity up just 0.9%. Compared to year-ago, productivity growth is z