\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Nationwide PharmAssist: We Weren't Part of Any Scam\b0\par\par\uc2 1,420 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2013\par 20:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:02 EDT - Nationwide PharmAssist, one of the Florida-based firms named in an SEC complaint against alleged penny-stock scammers earlier this week, says it wasn't involved in any such scheme. Ned Barnett, a spokesman for the small company, says Nationwide PharmAssist's founder, who was charged as an individual, resigned from the retail pharmacy in June. "We were not involved and we're confident that we're going to be dropped from the civil action," Mr. Barnett says. Scams involving dubious "penny stocks" often proliferate during stock-market booms like the recent rally. Lightly traded and typically bought and sold over the counter rather than on major exchanges, the shares are cheap and can be manipulated through sales pitches to investors and false rumors posted on message boards. (kristin.jones@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 When Treasury yields rise, riskier assets like stocks and emerging-market assets tend to shine as well. But not this time. As it is the prospect of Fed tapering that sparked a selloff in Treasury bonds, the higher yields have driven investors to sell US stocks as they prepare for a less generous Fed. No wonder the bond king has the blues. In a message Friday on Pimco's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account, Bill Gross says that all asset markets are peaking and that without \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  check writing, or pumping money from QEs, "we only have ourselves [to] sell [to]." (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 13:10 EDT - \uc2 Citadel LLC\uc2 , until earlier this year a major \uc2 E*Trade Financial\uc2  (ETFC) investor and sometime agitator there, sees no sale for the discount brokerage firm in next 24 months, according to research note from Sandler O'Neill recapping recent meeting with Citadel CEO \uc2 Ken Griffin\uc2 . Citadel's take: \uc2 Charles Schwab\uc2  (SCHW) isn't an ETFC suitor because SCHW is still integrating past deals, and any acquisition of ETFC by \uc2 TD Ameritrade\uc2  (AMTD) won't come until current TD Bank Chairman Ed Clark retires, currently slated for November 2014. Representatives for ETFC and AMTD decline comment; SCHW was not available. (jacob.bunge@wsj.com; @jacobbunge)\par \par \uc2 In a survey of its members, one of Venezuela's largest private-sector groups, Conindustria, estimates that the countries' industries are working at little more than half capacity. Among the main reasons given for the stunted output were political uncertainty, the difficulty in obtaining foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and raw materials, and power outages. President Nicolas Maduro, barely out of his first 100 days in office, began his term by reaching out to the private sector and naming cabinet members that promised more pragmatic policy. But political tension remains high and dollars are still scarce in the stalled economy. (ezequiel.minaya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Technical factors are coming into play on a thinned out Friday session, with few buyers around to stem the bleeding in US Treasurys. Benchmark 10-year yields just rose another 3bps, now at 2.851%, setting yet another 2-year high. Technical strategists have warned that breaching 2.75% would pave the way to faster selling. The next key support seen luring in buyers is just above 3%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Underscoring how sentiment toward bonds remains negative, Treasurys are getting hit with another round of selling after stabilizing this morning. The 10-year yield climbs as high as 2.829%, topping the 2-year high of 2.823% set Thursday. "The long-term trend is higher in Treasury yields," says Sean Simko, fixed-income manager at SEI Investments. "Anytime there is a strong move higher in Treasury yields, there will be certain investors that will reallocate assets out of bond funds." Simko says bond prices are looking more attractive in the short-term after this recent selloff. "If there is any inclination that the Fed may not taper in September, Treasury bonds could catch a strong bid," he adds. Ten-year notes down 16/32, yielding 2.825%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 12:19 EDT - InterOil (IOC) slips on a report from PNG Industry News citing a source saying that \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2 's (XOM) exclusive period of negotiations for a large stake of IOC's Elk-Antelope field in Papua New Guinea is over and that XOM failed to close a deal. Reps for IOC and XOM weren't immediately available to comment, but Raymond James analyst Pavel Molchanov says that while the shares are being weighed down by the report, PNG "has a lengthy track record of posting IOC-related rumors that end up being disproven by reality." Molchanov adds that even if XOM and IOC don't finalize a transaction, the fact that IOC "had multiple bidders indicates that there is no shortage of interest from other parties." IOC shares off lows, but still down 3% to $72.18. (anna.prior@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Pimco senior rate strategist Tony Crescenzi writes in his August missive three policy takeaways that will influence the bond market in the years to come. 1) It takes cumulative data for the Fed to act. 2) Tapering is not tightening. In fact, Crescenzi notes the expected $10B reduction in monthly MBS purchases will actually be less than MBS originations because of the recent plunge in mortgage refinancing activity. That means at $30B a month, the Fed would actually be buying a bigger proportion of the market. 3) QE and the policy rate are two separate tools. As investors grow to understand this, Crescenzi says rates will be tied more to the latter. Pimco see the Fed funds rate unchanged until early 2016. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to ease the depreciation on the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the US dollar, Banco de Credito said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in recent sessions, selling dollars on the spot market. So far this week, it has sold $620M to prevent the sol from weakening further. The sol has depreciated 9.6% against the greenback so far this year, with the dollar recently trading at PEN2.797. (ryan.dube@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:41 EDT - \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  upgrades Pandora (P) to buy saying three quarters of accelerating mobile ad growth show company's ability to monetize its large usage base. Firm is less worried about competition from rivals, saying P's "broad distribution ecosystem and large existing user base is underappreciated." Goldman expects P's revenue growth to continue to top its content acquisition cost growth, helped by its mobile hours cap. Wedbush remains neutral, but does expect P to raise guidance slightly when it reports earnings on Thursday and isn't worried about competition ahead of the iTunes Radio launch. P up 6.7% to $21.18. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 For the sixth-straight week, the amount of US Treasurys owned by foreign accounts stashed at the NY Fed fell. The $5.2B drop brings the total decline since early June to $58B. But RBS assures the streak shouldn't be interpreted as foreign investors losing confidence in \uc2 US government\uc2  bonds. Instead, the firm points out that these so-called custody holdings tend to track how Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  perform. When these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened amid growing Fed stimulus, dollars were bought to cap that strength, then often used to invest in US Treasurys. Now that EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are weakening, the process is reversing. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Peru's economic conditions are putting its policymakers between a rock and a hard place. The \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of Peru\uc2  faces decelerating economic growth, which points to a need for cut rates, while inflation and exchange-rate pressures may require a rate hike instead. "Facing these two opposite forces, we believe the most likely scenario is that the bank will remain on hold," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said. "We think an initial stimulus to contain the slowdown could be provided through additional reductions in reserve requirements." Last week, Peru left its policy rate at 4.25%, where it has stood since May 2011. (ryan.dube@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 16, 2013 14:02 ET (18:02 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130816e98g004dt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
