\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: \uc2 Peru  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Likely to Keep Supporting Sol\b0\par\par\uc2 1,376 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2013\par 16:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to ease the depreciation on the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the US dollar, Banco de Credito said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in recent sessions, selling dollars on the spot market. So far this week, it has sold $620M to prevent the sol from weakening further. The sol has depreciated 9.6% against the greenback so far this year, with the dollar recently trading at PEN2.797. (ryan.dube@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:41 EDT - \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  upgrades Pandora (P) to buy saying three quarters of accelerating mobile ad growth show company's ability to monetize its large usage base. Firm is less worried about competition from rivals, saying P's "broad distribution ecosystem and large existing user base is underappreciated." Goldman expects P's revenue growth to continue to top its content acquisition cost growth, helped by its mobile hours cap. Wedbush remains neutral, but does expect P to raise guidance slightly when it reports earnings on Thursday and isn't worried about competition ahead of the iTunes Radio launch. P up 6.7% to $21.18. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 For the sixth-straight week, the amount of US Treasurys owned by foreign accounts stashed at the NY Fed fell. The $5.2B drop brings the total decline since early June to $58B. But RBS assures the streak shouldn't be interpreted as foreign investors losing confidence in \uc2 US government\uc2  bonds. Instead, the firm points out that these so-called custody holdings tend to track how Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  perform. When these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened amid growing Fed stimulus, dollars were bought to cap that strength, then often used to invest in US Treasurys. Now that EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are weakening, the process is reversing. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Peru's economic conditions are putting its policymakers between a rock and a hard place. The \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of Peru\uc2  faces decelerating economic growth, which points to a need for cut rates, while inflation and exchange-rate pressures may require a rate hike instead. "Facing these two opposite forces, we believe the most likely scenario is that the bank will remain on hold," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said. "We think an initial stimulus to contain the slowdown could be provided through additional reductions in reserve requirements." Last week, Peru left its policy rate at 4.25%, where it has stood since May 2011. (ryan.dube@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Disappointing July housing starts earlier this morning and now a consumer sentiment report coming in below expectations aren't usually the makings of a dollar rally. Yet, the greenback is holding up against the yen, which shows how fervently markets believe an autumn tapering is coming. At this point, many market participants believe only a dismal August nonfarm payrolls number can derail a stimulus reduction. Dollar up 0.1% at Y97.48. Euro up 0.1% to $1.3355, but down from $1.3374 just before the sentiment report. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Bond prices hold losses, brushing aside a report that showed consumers feeling less confident this month. But the data doesn't change the prospect of less Fed stimulus, so anxiety over the Fed tapering next month continues to weigh on bonds. A key question now is if the Fed does taper in September, how much will it reduce monthly purchases by? Fed's Bullard floated the idea of a mini-tapering Thursday. If that turns out to be the case, bond bears might be forced to pull back negative bets on bonds, which would help Treasurys regain footing. Ten-year notes down 7/32, yielding 2.792%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 10:02 EDT - The S&P 500 dipped below the 50-day moving average intraday for the first time in six weeks, but technicians don't believe that's a significant bearish development. "In this particular case, it doesn't mean too much," said United-ICAP technical analyst Brian LaRose, since the drop below the 50-day MA in June didn't lead to much deeper losses. S&P 500 down 0.1% at 1660, off an intraday low of 1656.19; the 50-day MA is at 1657.46. That doesn't mean LaRose is bullish. He said it's too early say whether the long-term uptrend is in danger, but the market seems due "for more than just a minor correction." His next targets are 1640 and 1610. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real, which has been losing ground to the dollar since March, has more room to fall in the coming year, say \uc2 Barclays\uc2  strategists. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness arises from global and local factors. At home, Brazil's government remains reluctant to loosen up fiscal policy, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks as if it's no longer defending a specific exchange rate to help hold up the real, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  notes. All the while, if the Fed does reduce stimulus, less global money will be headed Brazil's way. This should put further pressure on BRL, which is currently trading at a four-year low to the dollar at BRL2.3535. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  sees the dollar rising to BRL2.45 within the next 12 months. (prabha.natarajan@wsj.com; @prabhanatarajan)\par \par \uc2 09:29 EDT - Homebuilder stocks are building on Thursday's bullish technical reversal patterns, even though data this morning showed housing starts rose less than expected last month. Pre-market, Lennar (LEN) up 1%, \uc2 PulteGroup\uc2  (PHM) up 0.6% and \uc2 D.R. Horton\uc2  (DHI) tacking on 0.7%. Last session, all three stocks hit one-year lows in intraday trading before reversing to close above Wednesday's intraday highs. Known as "key reversals," these patterns suggest a short-term bottom has been seen. The PHLX Housing Sector Index (HGX) also produced a "key reversal" Thursday, just in time to avoid a new bear market. The index was down 2.8% intraday, or 21% below its May closing high of 208.44, before recovering to close up 1.2%. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 The dollar falls to Y97.28 after July housing starts disappoint, from Y97.52 just before the data. But the move isn't a dramatic one--the market believes September tapering is still on the table because traders' faith won't be shaken by a miss in one piece of second-tier data. The miss, however, does contribute to the stagnation the dollar have been facing for the last few weeks: while the market is convinced of a Fed stimulus rollback in September, US data have been spotty and uneven, leaving the dollar and most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  adrift for the time being. Euro up 0.1% to $1.3365.(ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Downward revisions to 1Q and weak 2Q numbers mean productivity is hardly moving so far this year. New data (linked to GDP revisions) show 1Q output per hour in nonfarm business fell 1.7%, instead of a 0.5% gain as first reported, and 2Q productivity up just 0.9%. Compared to year-ago, productivity growth is zero for each quarter. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Both housing starts and building permits posted a smaller gain than economists forecast. For the moment, this lured some short-covering in bonds and pushed prices off session lows. Ten-year notes off 6/32 to yield 2.788%, from around 7.8% prior to the report. The dollar turns lower against the yen, last at Y97.37. The bond market, however, risks more downside amid rising anxiety over Fed tapering as soon as September. Any signs of global growth picking up could send the yield toward 3% in coming months and help boost the dollar. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 16, 2013 10:42 ET (14:42 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130816e98g003gb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
