\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: Expect Moves Higher In NZD/USD, AUD/USD -\uc2 Westpac\b0\par\par\uc2 1,375 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2013\par 22:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Expect moves higher in both the NZD/USD and the AUD/USD Tuesday, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Imre Speizer. Market attention will be on Australia's monthly business confidence and pre-election fiscal outlook, he says. If confidence continues to remain weak, that could put some pressure on the AUD, however Speizer says the AUD/USD is likely to continue its five-day upward correction in Tuesday's session, with the pair expected to head toward 0.9300. The pair is at 0.9149 vs 0.9177 late Monday. Speizer adds he expects similar moves in the NZD/USD, with the pair targeting 0.8100. The pair is at 0.8007 vs 0.8040. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Expect moves higher in both the NZD/USD and the AUD/USD Tuesday, says \uc2 Westpac\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Imre Speizer. Market attention will be on Australia's monthly business confidence and pre-election fiscal outlook, he says. If confidence continues to remain weak, that could put some pressure on the AUD, however Speizer says the AUD/USD is likely to continue its five-day upward correction in Tuesday's session, with the pair expected to head toward 0.9300. The pair is at 0.9149 vs 0.9177 late Monday. Speizer adds he expects similar moves in the NZD/USD, with the pair targeting 0.8100. The pair is at 0.8007 vs 0.8040. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 So much for Mexico galloping ahead of other emerging markets. Slowing growth, falling industrial production and decline in construction all signal an economy that is still struggling, Citi analysts say. It's a disappointment for many who had thought 2Q will usher in a rebound for Mexico, which had made it an investor favorite. Its economy is closely tied to the US and was expected to benefit from the renewed appetite of American consumers. But with a weak start to auto manufacturing and retail trade in July/August, Citi now expects 3Q to continue the weak trend. Firm now calling for 2% growth this year versus 2.7% projected previously. (prabha.natarajan@wsj.com; @prabhanatarajan)\par \par \uc2 So much for Mexico galloping ahead of other emerging markets. Slowing growth, falling industrial production and decline in construction all signal an economy that is still struggling, Citi analysts say. It's a disappointment for many who had thought 2Q will usher in a rebound for Mexico, which had made it an investor favorite. Its economy is closely tied to the US and was expected to benefit from the renewed appetite of American consumers. But with a weak start to auto manufacturing and retail trade in July/August, Citi now expects 3Q to continue the weak trend. Firm now calling for 2% growth this year versus 2.7% projected previously. (prabha.natarajan@wsj.com; @prabhanatarajan)\par \par \uc2 Nomura analysts are adding to their long USD/COP position in their emerging-markets \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  portfolio, a position they first recommended via 1-year NDF back in May. They warn that "the temporary rally observed in COP during July is coming to an end." That's because Fed Chairman Bernanke's dovish tone to ease market tensions and the increase in oil prices in July--both COP-supportive factors--"will disappear soon." Plus, they expect a decrease in dollar inflows to Colombia's economy, which will contribute to the peso's depreciation. USD/COP steady at 1872, according to CQG. (erin.mccarthy@wsj.com; @eemccarthy)\par \par \uc2 Nomura analysts are adding to their long USD/COP position in their emerging-markets \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  portfolio, a position they first recommended via 1-year NDF back in May. They warn that "the temporary rally observed in COP during July is coming to an end." That's because Fed Chairman Bernanke's dovish tone to ease market tensions and the increase in oil prices in July--both COP-supportive factors--"will disappear soon." Plus, they expect a decrease in dollar inflows to Colombia's economy, which will contribute to the peso's depreciation. USD/COP steady at 1872, according to CQG. (erin.mccarthy@wsj.com; @eemccarthy)\par \par \uc2 The dollar continues to notch gains versus the euro and yen in New York trading, which one \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a major investment bank attributes to a handful of investors in a thinly traded market paring back bets that the greenback would continue to slide. The moves don't necessarily represent a newfound bullishness about the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said, and with little economic data out today, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are generally quiet. Markets in the U.S. may heat up later in the week, however, with key retail sales, inflation, industrial production and housing data scheduled for release. Dollar is at Y96.58 from Y96.21, according to EBS via CQG. (matthew.walter@wsj.com; @mbwalter4)\par \par \uc2 The dollar continues to notch gains versus the euro and yen in New York trading, which one \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a major investment bank attributes to a handful of investors in a thinly traded market paring back bets that the greenback would continue to slide. The moves don't necessarily represent a newfound bullishness about the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said, and with little economic data out today, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are generally quiet. Markets in the U.S. may heat up later in the week, however, with key retail sales, inflation, industrial production and housing data scheduled for release. Dollar is at Y96.58 from Y96.21, according to EBS via CQG. (matthew.walter@wsj.com; @mbwalter4)\par \par \uc2 Mexican peso weakens against the U.S. dollar, to MXN12.6455 vs MXN12.5670 in morning trade, after President \uc2 Enrique Pena Nieto\uc2  outlined his proposed energy reform. The President emphasized that Mexico would retain ownership of its oil wealth under his plan, suggesting profit-sharing contracts with drillers but not production-sharing contracts. Markets were expecting something more aggressive, said Antonio Magana, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Mexican brokerage Interacciones. Yet the sell-off could be an over-reaction. "This still isn't the definitive" reform, he warned. (amy.guthrie@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Mexican peso weakens against the U.S. dollar, to MXN12.6455 vs MXN12.5670 in morning trade, after President \uc2 Enrique Pena Nieto\uc2  outlined his proposed energy reform. The President emphasized that Mexico would retain ownership of its oil wealth under his plan, suggesting profit-sharing contracts with drillers but not production-sharing contracts. Markets were expecting something more aggressive, said Antonio Magana, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Mexican brokerage Interacciones. Yet the sell-off could be an over-reaction. "This still isn't the definitive" reform, he warned. (amy.guthrie@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  say they "still believe Peru's government is determined in drawing a line at 2.80" for the Peruvian sol against the US dollar because policymakers don't feel comfortable with a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker than that. \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has consistently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market amid PEN weakness. "We expect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue as needed given the high dollarization of the economy: 48% of credit to the private sector is in dollars," BofA adds. And with inflation rising above its upper target range in July, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not tolerate much more PEN weakness. USD/PEN last up 0.1% at 2.796. (erin.mccarthy@wsj.com; @eemccarthy)\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  say they "still believe Peru's government is determined in drawing a line at 2.80" for the Peruvian sol against the US dollar because policymakers don't feel comfortable with a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker than that. \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has consistently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market amid PEN weakness. "We expect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue as needed given the high dollarization of the economy: 48% of credit to the private sector is in dollars," BofA adds. And with inflation rising above its upper target range in July, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not tolerate much more PEN weakness. USD/PEN last up 0.1% at 2.796. (erin.mccarthy@wsj.com; @eemccarthy) [ 12-08-13 2041GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130812e98c000pf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
