\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: Peru seen defending sol at around 2.80 per dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 1,475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2013\par 19:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  say they "still believe Peru's government is determined in drawing a line at 2.80" for the Peruvian sol against the US dollar because policymakers don't feel comfortable with a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker than that. \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has consistently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market amid PEN weakness. "We expect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue as needed given the high dollarization of the economy: 48% of credit to the private sector is in dollars," BofA adds. And with inflation rising above its upper target range in July, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not tolerate much more PEN weakness. USD/PEN last up 0.1% at 2.796. (erin.mccarthy@wsj.com; @eemccarthy)\par \par \uc2 Trading in emerging-market credit default swaps rose to $279B in 2Q, according to a survey of 13 major dealers taken by trade group EMTA. That volume is a 28% increase over the previous year and 31% increase over the previous quarter. Broad concerns about the health of emerging markets has increased CDS demand from hedgers and speculators, said Jane Brauer of \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 , though the increase in CDS trading was uneven across the globe. The EU's ban on speculative trades lowered the volumes from that region, while Asia and Latin America showed increases. Brazil represented the largest CDS volume last quarter at $65B. (prabha.natarajan@wsj.com; @prabhanatarajan)\par \par \uc2 Trading in emerging-market credit default swaps rose to $279B in 2Q, according to a survey of 13 major dealers taken by trade group EMTA. That volume is a 28% increase over the previous year and 31% increase over the previous quarter. Broad concerns about the health of emerging markets has increased CDS demand from hedgers and speculators, said Jane Brauer of \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 , though the increase in CDS trading was uneven across the globe. The EU's ban on speculative trades lowered the volumes from that region, while Asia and Latin America showed increases. Brazil represented the largest CDS volume last quarter at $65B. (prabha.natarajan@wsj.com; @prabhanatarajan)\par \par \uc2 Angel Garcia Banchs, the head of Venezuelan business consultancy Econometrica, believes that the country's system of dollar auctions, launched to pump hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the economy, will be a failed stopgap. Many had hoped the auction system would allow Venezuela's private sector to influence the exchange rate at which the auctioned dollars are awarded. Garcia says that with three auctions completed, it is clear that it's still "the state, arbitrarily, setting the rate." Many analysts have also noted that the auctions have provided fewer dollars than the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  mechanism it replaced earlier this year. (ezequiel.minaya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Angel Garcia Banchs, the head of Venezuelan business consultancy Econometrica, believes that the country's system of dollar auctions, launched to pump hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the economy, will be a failed stopgap. Many had hoped the auction system would allow Venezuela's private sector to influence the exchange rate at which the auctioned dollars are awarded. Garcia says that with three auctions completed, it is clear that it's still "the state, arbitrarily, setting the rate." Many analysts have also noted that the auctions have provided fewer dollars than the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  mechanism it replaced earlier this year. (ezequiel.minaya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 David Rosenberg, chief economist and strategist at Gluskin/Sheff, says there's a dearth of evidence to build a case for a collapse in Canada's housing market: existing home sales are running at double-digit rates year-over-year; average home prices ran up 5% from a year ago in June; and, despite the unexpected falloff in building permits in June, total ground-breakings in July totaled 193K annualized units, almost unchanged from June. "All in, it looks increasingly convincing to us that the there is no hard landing but stabilization in the Canadian housing market after the government ushered in more restrictive mortgage insurance rules aimed at curbing runaway household debt levels and cooling down the red-hot residential segment last year," he says. (don.curren@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 David Rosenberg, chief economist and strategist at Gluskin/Sheff, says there's a dearth of evidence to build a case for a collapse in Canada's housing market: existing home sales are running at double-digit rates year-over-year; average home prices ran up 5% from a year ago in June; and, despite the unexpected falloff in building permits in June, total ground-breakings in July totaled 193K annualized units, almost unchanged from June. "All in, it looks increasingly convincing to us that the there is no hard landing but stabilization in the Canadian housing market after the government ushered in more restrictive mortgage insurance rules aimed at curbing runaway household debt levels and cooling down the red-hot residential segment last year," he says. (don.curren@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Of all data releases, surprises in monthly nonfarm payrolls move bonds and stocks most. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  finds that a 1-standard-deviation surprise--about a 80K swing--causes an average 5-basis-point move in the 10-year yield and 0.3% impact on the S&P 500. But when it comes to predictive powers for how the economy will actually fare in the months ahead, the investment bank finds payrolls has little. Instead, the Philly Fed and ISM manufacturing reports best illustrate the economy's strength and momentum. Those reports are both more timely (released shortly after surveys are taken) and subject to smaller revisions, says Goldman. The latest data from both were also surprisingly upbeat, contrasting the underwhelming July jobs. August Philly Fed comes Thursday. (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Of all data releases, surprises in monthly nonfarm payrolls move bonds and stocks most. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  finds that a 1-standard-deviation surprise--about a 80K swing--causes an average 5-basis-point move in the 10-year yield and 0.3% impact on the S&P 500. But when it comes to predictive powers for how the economy will actually fare in the months ahead, the investment bank finds payrolls has little. Instead, the Philly Fed and ISM manufacturing reports best illustrate the economy's strength and momentum. Those reports are both more timely (released shortly after surveys are taken) and subject to smaller revisions, says Goldman. The latest data from both were also surprisingly upbeat, contrasting the underwhelming July jobs. August Philly Fed comes Thursday. (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in the middle of a holding pattern in its monetary policy that will likely last through the rest of 2013, says Nomura Securities. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark lending rate is at 3.25%, and will likely stay at that level as inflation remains subdued and there are mixed signals on the performance of the domestic economy, Nomura says. Minutes from the last meeting, which were released Friday, ratify this projection and also add to expectations that there will be a modest economic recovery in the coming quarters, firm says, adding that the bank likely starts rating hikes in the second half of 2014. (darcy.crowe@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in the middle of a holding pattern in its monetary policy that will likely last through the rest of 2013, says Nomura Securities. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark lending rate is at 3.25%, and will likely stay at that level as inflation remains subdued and there are mixed signals on the performance of the domestic economy, Nomura says. Minutes from the last meeting, which were released Friday, ratify this projection and also add to expectations that there will be a modest economic recovery in the coming quarters, firm says, adding that the bank likely starts rating hikes in the second half of 2014. (darcy.crowe@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 One group of investors are pumped up about rising bond yields. Dan Fuss, manager of the $21.7B \uc2 Loomis Sayles Bond Fund\uc2 , told WSJ in a recent interview that while the fund has suffered three straight monthly outflow through July, the firm's institutional business--primarily pension funds--has seen new cash coming in. "Higher yields are really phenomenally good news for pensions," Fuss said. Pension funds typically use the yield on investment-grade corporate bonds as the discount rate for their financial obligations. So the higher the yield, the smaller its liabilities. This comes as pension funds' assets have increased thanks to a strong rally in US stocks this year, though he warns that the risk now is a stock selloff sending bond yields back down. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 12, 2013 13:41 ET (17:41 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130812e98c0043m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
