\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Time to Consider Buying Utilities -Asbury Research\b0\par\par\uc2 1,437 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2013\par 21:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 15:56 EDT - Asbury Research director of research John Kosar says it's time to consider flipping to an overweight position on utilities, as he expects a pullback in Treasury yields. The SPDR Utilities ETF (XLU) down 1.3% at $37.87, bouncing off an intraday low of $37.46, which was just above the 200-day moving average at $37.12. The XLU has lost 8.6% this month amid rising yields. The XLU has reached "quarterly oversold extremes" versus the benchmark S&P 500. Previous similar conditions have led to intermediate-term periods of relative outperformance by utilities. He sees the 10-year yield, last at 2.123%, as overbought, and expects it to drop back below key support at 2.08% in the short term. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Upticks this week in Argentine dollar bonds make them some of the best performers in Latin America while many other emerging-market assets come under pressure. Siobhan Morden of Jefferies says these bonds offer the best of both worlds: They're largely immune from Argentina's local exchange-rate issues but also less vulnerable to US Treasurys selloffs since returns come largely from country-specific credit risk. Light positioning furthers their case. "Argentina is the perverse safe haven...because there is no leverage, there is no positioning," she adds. Argentina's Boden 2015 trades at 87.25 to yield 13.541, while the Global 2017 trades at 78.24 to yield 16.377, according to Markit. (erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 A big driver behind the selloff in Treasury has been MBS investors hedging against a further rise in yields. But Jim Caron, global fixed-income portfolio manager at \uc2 Morgan Stanley Investment Management\uc2 , believes "a lot of such MBS hedge selling has been done" and that "the rapid pace of rise in bond yields should diminish." For now, Caron argues that the Fed is not overly concerned about the rise in yields as the move is justified by stronger data. Only when the 10-year yield hits 2.5% would get the Fed worried, he says. Next Friday's nonfarm jobs report is key, he says, and a strong report would cement QE tapering fears and send bond yields higher. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 Upticks this week in Argentine dollar bonds make them some of the best performers in Latin America while many other emerging-market assets come under pressure. Siobhan Morden of Jefferies says these bonds offer the best of both worlds: They're largely immune from Argentina's local exchange-rate issues but also less vulnerable to US Treasurys selloffs since returns come largely from country-specific credit risk. Light positioning furthers their case. "Argentina is the perverse safe haven...because there is no leverage, there is no positioning," she adds. Argentina's Boden 2015 trades at 87.25 to yield 13.541, while the Global 2017 trades at 78.24 to yield 16.377, according to Markit. (erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's blistering selloff in Treasurys lifted trading in US debt-linked futures to record on markets run by CME, with 10.5M contracts changing hands. Ten-year yields are at their highest levels in more than a year, helping ignite CME's bigger interest-rate-futures complex--which has been sluggish with baseline US rates hovering near zero for years. Tuesday was the second-busiest day for CME's interest-rate-futures market, with 16.8M contracts traded. Amid today's broad market selloff, CME is up 3.6% at $67.82 in hitting a 3-year high. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 A big driver behind the selloff in Treasury has been MBS investors hedging against a further rise in yields. But Jim Caron, global fixed-income portfolio manager at \uc2 Morgan Stanley Investment Management\uc2 , believes "a lot of such MBS hedge selling has been done" and that "the rapid pace of rise in bond yields should diminish." For now, Caron argues that the Fed is not overly concerned about the rise in yields as the move is justified by stronger data. Only when the 10-year yield hits 2.5% would get the Fed worried, he says. Next Friday's nonfarm jobs report is key, he says, and a strong report would cement QE tapering fears and send bond yields higher. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's blistering selloff in Treasurys lifted trading in US debt-linked futures to record on markets run by CME, with 10.5M contracts changing hands. Ten-year yields are at their highest levels in more than a year, helping ignite CME's bigger interest-rate-futures complex--which has been sluggish with baseline US rates hovering near zero for years. Tuesday was the second-busiest day for CME's interest-rate-futures market, with 16.8M contracts traded. Amid today's broad market selloff, CME is up 3.6% at $67.82 in hitting a 3-year high. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Peru's sol has more room to depreciate against the US dollar, RBS says, although the \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease any volatility. "We estimate the exchange rate is still overvalued by close to 4% and expect it to remain under weakening pressure from the strong dollar trend in global capital markets," firm says. But relative to its neighboring \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , RBS sees the sol faring better as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's willingness to step in should limit its fall. US dollar last at PEN2.694, up 0.6%. (robert.kozak@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Peru's sol has more room to depreciate against the US dollar, RBS says, although the \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease any volatility. "We estimate the exchange rate is still overvalued by close to 4% and expect it to remain under weakening pressure from the strong dollar trend in global capital markets," firm says. But relative to its neighboring \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , RBS sees the sol faring better as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's willingness to step in should limit its fall. US dollar last at PEN2.694, up 0.6%. (robert.kozak@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 It may be premature to draw a line in the sand, but some believe the month-long selloff that hit a feverish pitch Tuesday for Treasurys is overdone, putting yields at attractive levels. "The recent selloff should not be viewed as the onset of an inexorable linear march to higher rates," says Eric Green at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 . "That may be the overriding theme over coming months and quarters, but the last leg in this recent selloff looks overdone." He cites a litany factors which should temper a rise in yields--including the Fed still keeping short-term policy rates near zero for a very long time, falling inflation and supply set to recede sharply thanks to fiscal tightening. It all suggest 10-year yields ending the year closer to 2% than 3%, says Green. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 It may be premature to draw a line in the sand, but some believe the month-long selloff that hit a feverish pitch Tuesday for Treasurys is overdone, putting yields at attractive levels. "The recent selloff should not be viewed as the onset of an inexorable linear march to higher rates," says Eric Green at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 . "That may be the overriding theme over coming months and quarters, but the last leg in this recent selloff looks overdone." He cites a litany factors which should temper a rise in yields--including the Fed still keeping short-term policy rates near zero for a very long time, falling inflation and supply set to recede sharply thanks to fiscal tightening. It all suggest 10-year yields ending the year closer to 2% than 3%, says Green. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 Desjardins Capital Markets says the general view on Canadian employment this year, based on the closely watched labor-force survey, is that job creation has been broadly weaker after a strong year 2012. The payroll employment survey, which comes almost two months after the LFS, provides a useful sanity check, and points to an even weaker performance, it says. The March payroll survey shows no jobs have been added in close to a year and the six-month pace of job creation dipping to negative, which has happened only twice before this century, firm says. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 29, 2013 15:56 ET (19:56 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130529e95t005ac\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
