\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Spoonful of reforms helps Latin America capital flows go down\b0\par\par\uc2 939 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 12:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Foreign inflows high for third year running in 2012\par \par \uc2 * Short-term policy response skips over reforms\par \par \uc2 * 'Perfect storm' exit not seen for now\par \par \uc2 By Krista Hughes\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, March 4 (Reuters) - B umper capital inflows to Latin America are putting the spotlight on shock-proofing policies to help economies digest the rush of investment - and guard against the inevitable exit.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investment inflows to Latin America were more than $280 billion in 2012, according to balance of payment data from countries covering 75 percent of the region's economic output, similar to the flows registered in 2011 as low interest rates in developed economies pushed investors to seek returns elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 According to the Institute of International Finance, Latin America outpaced other emerging market regions as a magnet for foreign capital in 2011 and 2012, taking the relative size of economies into account, a trend it expects to continue in 2013.\par \par \uc2 Rapid inflows can push up \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , cause domestic demand to overheat, create asset price bubbles and fan inflation, but can be offset by tighter fiscal policy and reforms to boost economic efficiency. Peru and Chile have already done much of their homework, but reforms are pending in Mexico and Brazil.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation pressures are a problem if they are too intense but it's also an opportunity to jump start structural reforms in order to improve productivity, which is one of the Achilles' heels of the region," said IIF Chief Latin America Economist Ramon Aracena.\par \par \uc2 Data show Peru and Chile are Latin America's foreign investment hotspots, with capital inflows, including banking, direct and portfolio investment, worth more than 10 percent of gross domestic product last year.\par \par \uc2 Mexico and Colombia follow, with inflows equivalent to around 7 percent of GDP, while Brazil - which has erected strict barriers to speculative financial investment - attracted inflows worth about 4 percent of GDP last year, down sharply.\par \par \uc2 SURGERY NOT PEPTO-BISMOL\par \par \uc2 Many countries have responded to the surge in investment with measures aimed at slowing inflows and preventing excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, ranging from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to taxes on foreign investment.\par \par \uc2 But such short-term measures are the policy equivalent of taking Pepto-Bismol when what is really required is abdominal surgery - deep reforms to make economies better able to absorb such inflows without suffering indigestion.\par \par \uc2 Policies to encourage productivity, a prudent fiscal stance, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility and adequate foreign reserves also help shield against disruptions when money flows out, although this is not expected until developed economies start raising rates.\par \par \uc2 Peru and Chile, which undertook a round of structural reforms in the 1990s, score best on a checklist covering these five factors. Of the inflation-targeting countries, Mexico is in third place, followed by Colombia and Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Running tight fiscal policy can help offset the boost to domestic demand from excess money sloshing around an economy, and gives the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  room to cut interest rates - making local assets less attractive to speculators.\par \par \uc2 Chile, the highest-rated sovereign issuer in Latin America, targets a structural budget balance, while Peru is the only major economy in the region that the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  expects to report an overall budget surplus in 2013.\par \par \uc2 A hands-off approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also allows the exchange rate to help balance the impact of inflows, dampening demand when inflows are high and providing a boost when flows reverse.\par \par \uc2 High levels of foreign reserves give countries a buffer against sudden capital reversals and room to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed. \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the largest foreign reserves as a share of economic output at more than 30 percent of GDP, double the level of Brazil, Chile and Mexico.\par \par \uc2 High productivity is the ultimate mark of a flexible economy, but Latin America has lagged other emerging market regions - especially Asia - in improving output efficiency.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Inter-American Development Bank\uc2  estimates that if productivity in the region were as high as in the United States, per capita income would double.\par \par \uc2 PERFECT STORM?\par \par \uc2 Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor \uc2 Agustin Carstens\uc2 , who has welcomed plans for structural reforms by the country's new government, warned last month that a "perfect storm" may be brewing as a result of the massive inflows to emerging markets, setting the stage for a painful reverse.\par \par \uc2 Thomson Reuters' Lipper service data show $495 million flowed out of U.S.-based Latin American equity funds last week, the biggest weekly fall since 2007, and emerging market debt also suffered as investors worried about Italy's deadlocked election result.\par \par \uc2 Funds tracker EPFR said stocks in Brazil, where the Bovespa index has slumped 10 percent since the start of the year, took the biggest hit in the recent outflow.\par \par \uc2 Many investors have been put off by Brazil's ad hoc capital controls and a failure to undertake deep reforms that would boost growth, although the country has recently started to unwind barriers to foreign investment, for example scrapping a 6 percent tax on purchases of real estate investment trusts.\par \par \uc2 Veteran emerging markets investor \uc2 Mark Mobius\uc2 , who oversees $51 billion in assets at funds manager Franklin Templeton, said he did not see bubbles at risk of bursting - yet.\par \par \uc2 "You'll know it when everyone is optimistic, everybody is buying and nobody is selling, and when the guy on the street says 'you should be buying stocks,' that's when you know you're in a bubble," he said during a visit to Mexico last week.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Krista Hughes; Editing by Maureen Bavdek)\par \par \uc2 LATINAMERICA-ECONOMY/ (ANALYSIS) | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | ABX | BNX | FUN | SP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130307e934000ou\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
