\par\par \b \uc2 Peru Expanding Efforts to Prevent  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Appreciation\b0\par\par\uc2 958 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2013\par 18:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --Government to buy up to $4 billion to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to allow pension funds to invest more abroad\par \par \uc2 --Creation of a sovereign wealth fund is under consideration\par \par \uc2 Peru is planning to step up its efforts to stem the appreciation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sol, as the country comes under increased pressure from capital flows leaving the developed world in search of more attractive emerging markets, according to interviews with top officials in the finance ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The government is planning to spend up to $4 billion in the first half of this year to buy dollars that will be used to prepay debt owed to multilateral lenders and add funds to the country's fiscal stabilization fund, Finance Minister Luis Miguel Castilla said. The country also is considering the creation of a sovereign wealth fund to save more of its surpluses outside the country, delaying future dollar bond sales and increasing the amount private pension funds can invest abroad.\par \par \uc2 While Peru's mining-led economy is forecast to expand at an enviable pace of 6.3% this year, with relatively stable inflation and healthy government accounts, business owners are concerned that the rapid appreciation of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may sap competitiveness from its export sector, undercutting growth in the future.\par \par \uc2 Officials have taken steps to prevent a surge of capital inflows from overheating the economy and driving the sol even higher. Most aggressively, the \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  actively in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, buying $13.2 billion in 2012 alone.\par \par \uc2 "What we've seen in the past few months is a faster rate of appreciation" in the sol, Mr. Castilla said in an interview in New York. "I think it's becoming too strong," though he said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value isn't yet too far out of alignment with its fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 Peru is one of many emerging-market countries concerned about efforts to stimulate growth in the U.S. and Japan through bond buying, which floods the world market with dollars and yen, pushing up the value of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Brazilian Finance Minister Guido Mantega first warned in 2010 that developing countries faced a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war," and the issue has resurfaced in recent months, as the Federal Reserve and Bank of Japan announced new plans to expand their bond-buying programs.\par \par \uc2 "I'm concerned about these very, very expansionary monetary policies, because they haven't taken into consideration the effect that this has on emerging markets," Mr. Castilla said. "We already had a trend towards capital inflows coming to emerging markets, but this has been exacerbated by the search for yield."\par \par \uc2 The sol has gain 4.8% in the past 12 months and is currently trading at PEN2.56 per dollar, near its strongest level in about 15 years. A stronger sol reduces the amount that the country earns, in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  terms, from its substantial copper, gold, zinc and silver exports. It also cuts into profits for exporters of manufactured goods, which have to lower their prices to compete.\par \par \uc2 The government plans to prepay up to $2 billion in debt owed to the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  and \uc2 Inter-American Development Bank\uc2  in the first quarter of this year, Mr. Castilla said. The payment likely will be financed in part by issuing local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt that will be exchanged for dollars, augmenting efforts at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the sol through \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mr. Castilla said. The government also will use funds from the treasury, he said.\par \par \uc2 Peru is planning to buy another $2 billion to replenish the country's fiscal stabilization fund during the first half of the year and for debt servicing. In a separate effort to mitigate pressure on the sol, Mr. Castilla said he had asked state-owned companies, including wholesale bank Cofide, to delay international bond sales that would increase the flow of dollars into the country. Cofide had planned to sell up to $1.5 billion in dollar bonds this year, but it may issue local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt in tranches instead, Mr. Castilla said.\par \par \uc2 In a separate interview, \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of Peru\uc2  Governor Julio Velarde said the sol was "slightly" overvalued at its current level and policymakers would keep a close watch on its strength.\par \par \uc2 He said he expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue to increase the amount of money that private pension funds can invest abroad by a rate of about two percentage points a quarter until it reaches the 50% legal limit, though the rate of increases will depend on how the economy and exchange rate evolve.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently increased that limit to 32% from 30%.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Velarde also said that he expects inflation to fall to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2% target by the end of 2013, which will make a rate cut possible this year, though he cautioned that any decision to loosen monetary policy will depend on how the economy performs going forward.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept its reference interest rate unchanged at 4.25% since May 2011.\par \par \uc2 Despite the challenges presented by the stronger sol, both Mr. Velarde and Mr. Castilla said they were broadly optimistic about the outlook, and the finance minister added that he may raise the country's growth forecast for this year.\par \par \uc2 "The numbers are going to be good" in the coming years, Mr. Velarde said. "Obviously, when the numbers are good, that's when we attract capital, which can then be a problem."\par \par \uc2 --Robert Kozak contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Matthew Walter at matthew.walter@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires }}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 29, 2013 12:45 ET (17:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130129e91t0062m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
