\par\par \b \uc2 Peru Ex-Trade Minister Says No Sol Policy Changes Needed for Exporters\b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert Kozak  \par \uc2 229 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2012\par 21:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 AREQUIPA, Peru--Former Trade Minister Alfredo Ferrero said Thursday the strength of Peru's sol, which has appreciated 4.45% so far this year against the U.S. dollar, doesn't appear to be harming exports from the Andean nation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 "Exports would be affected by keeping the sol too strong, and it is also the case that fixing the sol in an arbitrary manner would be a risk for the nation. The sol is strong because the dollar is weak on a global level, and that doesn't depend on Peru," he said on the sidelines of a business conference being held in this southern Peruvian city.\par \par \uc2 "I would say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should continue with its policies, and there is no reason for that to affect the exchange rate," he added.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  manages its floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a daily basis in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to purchase greenbacks. It has purchased some $12 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 A number of Peruvian exporters have complained that the sol's strength has hurt their exports.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ferrero was Trade and Tourism Minister in the administration of former President Alejandro Toledo and helped negotiate free-trade pacts with other nations.\par \par \uc2 Write to Robert Kozak at robert.kozak@dowjones.com [ 11-29-12 1524ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020121129e8bt000l4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
