\par\par \b \uc2 Capital Inflows Into Peru Seen Driving Sol's Continuing Appreciation\b0\par\par\uc2 466 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2012\par 22:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Appreciation of Peru's sol should continue next year\par \par \uc2 --Fundamental reason for gains shifts to capital inflows\par \par \uc2 --Foreign direct investment may hit $10 billion for 2012\par \par \uc2 By Robert Kozak\par \par \uc2 LIMA, Peru--Strength in Peru's sol is expected to continue next year, though it will be capital inflows driving most of the gains this time, say \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors.\par \par \uc2 Among those forecasting more gains is Scotiabank, the third-largest bank in Peru, which said low interest rates abroad, abundant liquidity and an improvement in Peru's risk profile are helping to attract capital. The bank forecasts record foreign direct investment this year of close to $10 billion as well as increased corporate and financial sector long-term loans and bond placements.\par \par \uc2 Drivers of previous rallies had been a positive trade balance and the process of domestic dedollarization.\par \par \uc2 "External noise is having less of an impact on the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than it did in the past. This shift, from being a high-risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a low-risk one, is structural," the bank said.\par \par \uc2 Already this year, the sol has appreciated 3.82% against the U.S. dollar, ending Wednesday at PEN2.594 per dollar. Scotiabank forecasts the sol will end 2013 at PEN2.49 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Other forecasters seeing gains are Capital Economics and \uc2 UBS\uc2 . \uc2 UBS\uc2  said it expects the Peruvian economy will expand by about 6.0% next year, while inflation is seen slowing toward 2.0%.\par \par \uc2 "We think the sol has room to strengthen but at a more-moderate pace," \uc2 UBS\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 One risk to further gains, however, is the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to purchase dollars. The \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to purchase U.S. dollars, having bought about $12 billion so far this year as part of its efforts to manage the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Another potential hit to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be if the government allows local private pension funds to invest more abroad.\par \par \uc2 Unlike other central banks around the world, which have moved aggressively to stem a drop in their home countries' economic prospects, \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has avoided changing its reference interest rate since May, 2011. It has even aimed to tighten credit this year by increasing reserve requirements.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main objective seems to be to maintain credibility in the context of ongoing strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  spot market to lean against sol appreciation," \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  said in a report, while adding that, "We remain of the view that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep the policy rate on hold for the foreseeable future."\par \par \uc2 Write to Robert Kozak at robert.kozak@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2  [ 21-11-12 2126GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument EM00000020121121e8bl00019\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
