\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's Main Stock Indexes Finish Lower; Sol Weaker\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ryan Dube   \par \uc2 239 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2012\par 22:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LIMA, Peru--Peru's main stock market indexes closed lower Wednesday, following a sharp decline on Wall Street after the U.S. presidential election and investor concerns about that country's fiscal cliff.\par \par \uc2 The Lima Stock Exchange's General Index dropped 0.24% to end at 20886.62\par \par \uc2 The blue-chip Selective Index ended 0.39% weaker at 30917.75\par \par \uc2 Financial-holding company \uc2 Credicorp Ltd\uc2 . (BAP, BAP.VL) lost 1.73% to end at $130.70. Credicorp owns Peru's biggest bank, \uc2 Banco de Credito\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Financial holding \uc2 Intercorp Financial Services Inc\uc2 . (IFS.VL) closed 1.39% lower at $32. The company owns Interbank, the country's fourth-largest bank.\par \par \uc2 Base metals-mining company \uc2 Southern Copper Corp\uc2 . (SCCO) declined 1.64% to end at $34.82.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Wednesday to buy $20 million at an average rate of 2.6069 Peruvian soles to the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to purchase U.S. dollars on a regular basis, in part to reverse an ongoing appreciation trend in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The sol closed weaker Wednesday at PEN2.608 to the dollar, compared with PEN2.599 the previous session.\par \par \uc2 So far this year, the sol has strengthened 3.3% against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ryan Dube at ryan.dube@dowjones.com [ 11-07-12 1613ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020121107e8b70012k\par }