\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's Sol Depreciates Following Regulatory Proposals -Nomura\b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert Kozak   \par \uc2 387 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2012\par 23:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LIMA, Peru--Peru's sol has been giving up strength against the U.S. dollar in recent sessions following changes to regulations made by a government regulator, and that depreciation may continue.\par \par \uc2 In recent sessions, the sol has appreciated about 1.0% against the greenback, ending Friday at PEN2.599 per dollar, compared to PEN2.590 in the previous session. The sol has appreciated 3.63% against the dollar this year.\par \par \uc2 Nomura strategist Benito Berber wrote in a report Friday that the sol could "actually depreciate a bit more given the changes in regulation."\par \par \uc2 He noted that the banking regulator will limit the net short U.S. dollar global position in both spot and nondelivery forwards of local financial intermediaries to 10% from 15% of their liquid assets, forcing the financial institutions to increase their dollar positions.\par \par \uc2 The regulator will also limit the nondelivery forwards position of financial institutions to 20% from 25% of their liquid asset position or below 300 million soles, ($115 million) whichever is a larger sum, aiming to reduce the exposure of local institutions to foreign flows, Nomura said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to purchase greenbacks in each trading session, whether the sol has been appreciating or depreciating. The goal is to take some of the certainty out of what had become a one-way bet on sol gains.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has purchased about $11.8 billion this year, driving Peru's net international reserves to a record $62.18 billion as of Oct. 23.\par \par \uc2 Nomura, while ruling out an increase in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reference interest rate next month, said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could increase reserve requirements next week to coincide with a long weekend, as it has done in the past.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has avoided increasing its policy rate, in part to avoid putting more appreciation pressure on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , economists say. The policy rate has remained unchanged at 4.25% since May 2011.\par \par \uc2 "In sum, the measures from the Peruvian authorities to limit the sol appreciation might actually put undesired depreciative pressure on the sol particularly if risk aversion intensifies," Nomura's Berber said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Robert Kozak at tobert.kozak@dowjones.com [ 10-26-12 1721ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020121026e8aq000u3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
