\par\par \uc2 Analysis\par \b \uc2 As Fed Readies QE3, Foreign Governments Brace for  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Onslaught\b0\par\par\uc2 By Erin McCarthy and Stephen L. Bernard  \par \uc2 1,561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2012\par 20:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top Global Market Stories\par \uc2 DJTGMS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --As with QE2, a weaker dollar is predicted for QE3\par \par \uc2 --Emerging market central banks might retaliate by weakening their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but that could stoke inflation\par \par \uc2 --The risk is that this descends into an-out race to the bottom with tit-for-tat \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciations\par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve's plan to embark on an open-ended round of asset purchases to bolster the U.S. economy has put financial authorities in other countries on high alert.\par \par \uc2 Looking back to the last time the Fed printed dollars to finance bond buying, which sent the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a year-long decline against most of its counterparts, officials are bracing for an unwanted flood of speculative money into their markets and a possible cycle of tit-for-tat retaliatory measures. The biggest fear is that it triggers a self-reinforcing and self-destructive race to the bottom in which monetary policy and direct \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are used to prevent other countries from gaining a competitive advantage through weaker exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan provided fodder for such concerns Wednesday. Just six days after the Fed said it would purchase an additional $40 billion in mortgage bonds a month for an indefinite period, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it would add another 10 trillion yen ($127 billion) to its asset-buying program and extend it out to the end of 2013. Although the BOJ didn't explicitly cite the yen's strength as a motivating factor, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately fell to a one-month low versus the dollar. Last Friday, following the Fed's announcement a day earlier, Finance Minister Jun Azumi forcefully complained that the strong yen was hurting the Japanese economy and hinted at direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by vowing to "take decisive steps when it is deemed necessary."\par \par \uc2 Some emerging market governments have also taken matters into their own hands this past week. Brazil has conducted so-called "reverse dollar swaps" to prevent its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, from appreciating; \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjusted its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy toward weakening the Peruvian sol; and Turkey cut interest rates by more than expected.\par \par \uc2 Others have been notably more muted in their response. Officials in South Korea, Thailand, Singapore and the Philippines took a cautiously sanguine view of the uptick in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though they all asserted a readiness to smooth out market movement if capital inflows become excessive.\par \par \uc2 The moves from the Fed and BOJ come after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced its own plan to buy bonds, in its case those of struggling governments in the periphery of the euro zone, in a bid to contain the region's debt crisis. Unlike the Fed and BOJ's actions, which will result in a net addition of fresh \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into global financial markets and are widely described as "quantitative easing," or QE, the ECB will "sterilize" its transactions by selling certificates that mop up the additional euros. Nonetheless, the moves are seen boosting the willingness of investors to take on risky investments generally, which typically results in large-scale transfers of funds out of dollars and into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "All of this cash generated by the Federal Reserve is going to be entering foreign shores," said Komal Sri-Kumar, chief global strategist at TCW. "Emerging markets are going to be tempted to cut interest rates...to offset their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciating too much."\par \par \uc2 Emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are in the crosshairs of speculators during such times because their interest rates tend to be higher than those in the U.S., European countries and Japan, allowing investors to book a profit on the difference between the two. But such countries often depend heavily on exports, which become more expensive when exchange rates rise. The dilemma for their central banks at times when global liquidity is rising is that they also face inflationary pressures from rising commodity prices and inflows into their real estate and other markets. If they were to combat those rising prices with higher rates, it would only further increase the appeal of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the previous round of Fed quantitative easing, which was dubbed "QE2," the dollar weakened significantly. In the 13 months from June 2010, when expectations of more Fed stimulus first began to rise, until the $600 billion bond-buying program wound up the following summer, the Wall Street Journal Dollar Index, a measure of the dollar's value against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , lost 18% of its value. It dropped 20% against the Brazilian real over the same period and 18% versus the Korean won.\par \par \uc2 The dollar followed a similar, but slower path leading up to the QE3 announcement last week. The WSJ Dollar Index hit a 22-month high in July and then started to slide gradually before dropping sharply once Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke signaled the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plans at his speech in Jackson Hole, Wyo, on Aug. 31. The index is now nearly 6% off its July high.\par \par \uc2 The fallout during QE2 in 2010 and 2011 prompted an especially bellicose response from a few foreign officials, with Brazilian Finance Minister Guido Mantega famously declaring that the world had entered a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war," an allusion to the highly damaging competitive-depreciation policies that undermined world trade during the Great Depression. This time around, Mr. Mantega has been less bellicose, though he remains critical of Fed policy. Speaking in Paris on Tuesday, he said, "I don't believe that the new monetary easing will resolve many problems for the U.S., but it could cause several problems for emerging market countries."\par \par \uc2 The Fed declined to comment Wednesday. In the past, Fed officials have defended their actions by saying that efforts to strengthen the U.S. recovery help the global economy. Mr. Bernanke said in 2010 that different monetary policies may be appropriate for different groups of countries, drawing a distinction between advanced economies and countries with emerging markets that may see more inflation pressure. But he also noted that disparities between exchange rate policies had led to global spending imbalances as "those countries that have allowed their exchange rates to be determined primarily by market forces have seen their competitiveness erode relative to countries that have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively."\par \par \uc2 In general, the latest response from other emerging market governments has been muted. That may be because those countries' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are already well down from their 2011 highs, a function in part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions many undertook in the wake of QE2, with Brazil in particular resorting both to dollar purchases and taxes on short-term foreign investment.\par \par \uc2 Yet this hands-off response could merely embolden foreign investors. Paresh Upadhyaya, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Pioneer Investments with $200 billion under management, said his firm has increased bets on the Korean won, Indian rupee, and Turkish lira over the past week, on expectations they'll benefit from monetary easing in the U.S, Japan, U.K. and euro zone. He says he doesn't expect as much of a backlash from emerging markets as there was during QE2.\par \par \uc2 The stakes may even be higher this time around. A much slower global growth environment and weakening international trade will make export-driven emerging market countries more protective of market share. And unlike the time limits that were placed on previous such programs, the Fed's new QE plan is open-ended, allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to effectively print money until the employment picture in the U.S. meaningfully improves.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, other major central banks beyond the Fed, ECB and BOJ have programs in place that tend to steer money away from their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Swiss National Bank has been regularly entering the market to buy euros and sell francs since September 2011, setting a floor on the pair. And the Bank of England has conducted Fed-style "unsterilized" bond-buying that pumps extra pounds into financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Faced with such barriers, investors will tend to go for higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are allowed to float more freely.\par \par \uc2 "Within the EM universe there clearly are going to be relative value trades," said Jonathon Griggs, global chief investment officer of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  group at J.P. Morgan Asset Management. In choosing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from "laissez-faire" countries over those that are more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mr. Griggs said one interesting trade is the Mexican peso against the Korean won. Other investors cite the Polish zloty, Norwegian krone and Canadian dollar.\par \par \uc2 The problem for policy makers in such countries, however, is that with so many other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  off limits, theirs could end up bearing an even bigger burden from exchange rate appreciation.\par \par \uc2 And if China, the world's biggest goods exporter, gets into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation game--either through direct moves to weaken the yuan or through a more aggressive round of monetary easing--the temptation for other countries to retaliate with their own actions could become even more intense.\par \par \uc2 "Three of the four major central banks have now acted and the last shoe to drop will be China," said James Rickards, partner at JAC Capital and author of the 2011 book "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wars: The Making of the Next Global Crisis."\par \par \uc2 --Matthew Walter and Kristina Peterson contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 -Write to Erin McCarthy at Erin.McCarthy@dowjones.com and Stephen L. Bernard at stephen.bernard@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTGMS1120120920e89k000au\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
